{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., zero payoff) compared to favorable outcomes (i.e., positive payoff). In this case, both lotteries have a zero outcome with high probability. Lottery B has a 67% chance of 0 Euros, while Lottery A has a 66% chance of 0 Euros. Thus, Lottery B has a higher probability of the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros), meaning it is more right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery. Therefore, the reason can be applied: the relevant information (probabilities of zero payoff and the associated outcomes) is present and directly comparable in the lotteries.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'ansehen aller Infos bei B war die Wahrscheinlichkeit für Null einen Prozentpunkt niedriger plus der mögliche Gewinn war 100€ höher'. This translates to: 'Looking at all the information, for B the probability of zero was one percentage point lower and the possible gain was 100€ higher.' This is inconsistent with the original data. In reality, Lottery B has a 67% chance of 0 Euros, while Lottery A has 66%. So B actually has a higher probability of zero (67% vs 66%), not lower. The report incorrectly states that B has a lower probability of zero. Additionally, the report emphasizes that B has a higher possible gain (2500 vs 2400), and that the probability of zero is lower by one percentage point — this is factually wrong. The individual appears to be misremembering or misrepresenting the probabilities. The justification offered is based on a mistaken comparison of probabilities and a focus on the gain difference, not on skewness or the distribution of probabilities toward unfavorable outcomes. Thus, the individual does not mention skewness or the relative probability mass on zero versus positive outcomes, and instead focuses on a misstated probability difference and a gain difference.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, the probability mass on unfavorable (zero) outcomes. The individual's report incorrectly states that B has a lower probability of zero (which is false; B has 67%, A has 66%), and instead focuses on a higher potential gain and a mistaken probability comparison. The individual does not consider or reference the skewness of the lotteries, nor does the justification reflect the rule that prefers the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., the one with more probability mass on zero). Instead, the reasoning is based on a misremembered factual detail and a focus on gain differences, not on distributional skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

